<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#serfdom">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Serfdom</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Serfdom was a form of unfree labour in which peasants were bound to the land and owed labour or dues to a lord. It was widespread in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and lasted longest in central and eastern Europe. It features in the profiles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, where it intensified with grain exports, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, where it was abolished in 1861.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/serfdom.jpg" alt="Serfdom" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous (Queen Mary Master) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reeve_and_Serfs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…set by treaties: the March Articles of 1654, the Pereiaslav Articles of 1659 that opened the way to <b>serfdom</b>, the Hlukhiv Articles of 1669 and the Konotop Articles of 1672, which removed the right to an independent foreign policy and took the election of the hetman from ordinary…</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Society came to resemble that of other Frankish lands: a warrior aristocracy of new families such as the Beaumonts, Montgomerys and Tosnys held lands from the duke, while peasants worked the soil in a province where <b>serfdom</b> seems to have faded comparatively early.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Confederation covered a society that was still largely rural, with <b>serfdom</b> and seigneurial dues being dismantled at different speeds from state to state, and with a population that grew from roughly 30 million to well over 40 million by the 1860s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1557 the Volok Reform of Sigismund II Augustus reorganised royal estates into standard plots, increasing grain output but tightening <b>serfdom</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the eighteenth century Maria Theresa’s Urbarium of 1767 regulated Hungarian peasant obligations, and Joseph II abolished personal <b>serfdom</b> in the Bohemian lands in 1781 and in Austria in 1782, though labour dues on the land remained.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Serfdom</b> and manorial obligation predominated across rural territories, with peasant burdens varying enormously by region and lord.</blockquote><a href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
